          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My dad is 80, confused since this morning and has not passed water since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what would be looked for?",
          "answer": "**This needs emergency care now. Call your local emergency number or take your father to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait for a routine appointment.**\n\nThe risk tool returned \"routine\" only because none of its fixed patterns matched this wording. That is not a clearance. The sepsis guidance I retrieved (red-flag-sepsis) says that confusion, or not passing urine for many hours, in someone with a known or suspected infection warrants emergency assessment. Your father has both. New confusion in an 80-year-old also needs prompt emergency assessment, because it can signal several serious conditions, including stroke. The hypertension guidance I retrieved lists confusion as an emergency when it comes with a very high blood pressure reading. I am treating this as an emergency because missing one would be far more harmful than acting on a false alarm.\n\n**Red flags in what you describe:**\n- New confusion that started this morning.\n- No urine since yesterday, which the sepsis guidance lists as a warning sign.\n\n**While you wait for help:**\n- Stay with him and do not leave him alone.\n- If he is drowsy or hard to wake, or has trouble breathing, weakness on one side, or trouble speaking, say so to the emergency call handler.\n- Take a list of his medicines and any recent illnesses or falls with you.\n\n**What clinicians check:** The passages I could retrieve did not cover the causes you asked about, such as urinary retention, dehydration, kidney or salt problems, stroke, and medicine effects. I could not check a source for those, so I won't list them here. The emergency team will assess them, and the list of medicines you bring may help them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
